Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report  ML 10  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   CASE  ML 10  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME   Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  MGH Inpatient    Consulting Pathologist s   None  Assigned Resident Path  Miguel N Rivera  M D   Clinical History     Reports To   KAREN KUHN BALLEN  M D         FINAL DIAGNOSIS     TEST PERFORMED    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ITD  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Mutation Analysis    INDICATION FOR TEST   Pancytopenia  suspect leukemia    RESULTS    NEGATIVE FOR  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ITD  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  AND  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  MUTATIONS    No  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  internal tandem duplication  ITD  or mutation at  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  was  detected in DNA extracted from this patient s bone marrow     No  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  exon 12 insertional mutations were detected     COMMENT  A low tumor burden  less than 10 tumor cells out of 100 cells  may  result in a false negative result  Please see test information below for  limitations     TEST INFORMATION    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ITD and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   DNA sequence insertions in the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  juxtamembrane domain   termed internal tandem duplication or ITD  or point mutations at nucleotides  encoding amino acids  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  in the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  activation loop have been found in  approximately 20 30  and 5 10   respectively  of AML  The presence of ITD  mutations have been associated with a poorer prognosis compared to  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   wild type tumors  the presence of the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutation has been shown in some  studies to portend a worse prognosis  although conflicting data remains in the  literature  1  2  3  4  5       REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutation  Mutations in the nucleophosmin gene   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   are usually small  insertions  typically 4 bp  sometimes up to 11 bp  and usually heterozygous  in  the coding region of the terminal exon 12  occurring in about 50 60  of  cytogenetically normal AMLs or about 20 25  of all AMLs  A number of studies  have evaluated the association of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutations and prognosis in AML and  demonstrated statistically significant favorable disease outcome particularly  in patients carrying the mutation but lacking  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutations  1  5  6  7  8   9       A multiplex  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ITD and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  exon 12 PCR assay to detect the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ITD and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   insertional mutations based on size was performed with genomic DNA samples from  blood or bone marrow  The analytical sensitivity of detecting these mutations  has been established in our laboratory at approximately 5  allele frequency or  10  mutant cells with heterozygous  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ITD or  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  insertional mutations in a  background of 90  normal cells     We have developed a single base extension assay  SNaPshot  to genotype four  bases in a portion of exon 20 from the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  gene which encode codons  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   c  REMOVED_CASE_ID G  c  REMOVED_CASE_ID A  c  REMOVED_CASE_ID T  and c  REMOVED_CASE_ID C   RefSeq NM_ REMOVED_CASE_ID  10    Genotyping is achieved in either the forward or reverse directions by single  base extension  Testing is performed on genomic DNA samples from blood or bone  marrow  The analytical sensitivity of detecting the mutation has been  established in our laboratory at approximately 1  mutant allele frequency or 2   heterozygous mutant cells for the most common c  REMOVED_CASE_ID G T mutation  Our assay  should pick up all point mutations in codon 835  GAT  and common mutations in  codon 836  e g   c  REMOVED_CASE_ID C G  c  REMOVED_