Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  18 REMOVED_MONTH 92 ACCESSIONED ON  18 REMOVED_MONTH 92 at 11 50 CLINICAL DATA  Postmenopausal female with a 1 to 2cm round mobile mass in the medial portion of the right breast  No clinical axillary or supraclavicular adenopathy  A recent needle aspiration in office was interpreted as suspicious for carcinoma                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  1  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE 1 OF 3  AND DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  WITH FEATURES OF NEUROENDOCRINE DIFFERENTIATION  SEE NOTE  2  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES INCLUDING FIBROSIS  CYSTS  APOCRINE METAPLASIA  SCLEROSING ADENOSIS  AND MODERATE DUCTAL EPITHELIAL HYPERPLASIA WITH FOCAL ATYPICALITY  3  MICROCALCIFICATIONS  NOTE  No definite lymphatic and vascular invasion is identified  The tumor measures 1 5cm in greatest dimension and is less than 0 1cm from several inked margins  but because of its unusual architectural features  the relative quantities of the invasive and in situ components cannot be adequately assessed  Ductal carcinoma in situ is present at a distance of at least one low power microscopic field from the main tumor mass  Using immunoperoxidase methods on paraffin embedded  formalin fixed tissue  many tumor cells are positive for carcinoembryonic antigen  rare tumor cells are positive for synaptophysin  suggesting neuroendocrine differentiation  and tumor cells are negative for chromogranin  Tumor cells do not show argyrophil granules by the Grimelius method  Dr  Koerner has reviewed this case and agrees with the diagnosis  Unequivocal invasive carcinoma is not identified on the frozen section slide  Diagnosis by  John H  Eichhorn  M D  Signed On  07 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 16 28                                                                                     RECEPTO