t IDC S P bilateral mastectomy  TRAM flap and  radiation  Recent painful left chest wall nodule     Review of constitutional symptoms  generally feeling well     Physical Exam  Constitutional  The patient appeared well developed   well nourished  and in no acute distress     The left chest wall showed a 0 5cm tender  well defined  round and firm nodule     Ultrasound attempted to evaluate the lesion but could not be visualized     Assessment  Based on the clinical history and physical examination  an FNAB is  warranted     Procedure  The procedure and potential complications were explained to the  patient  who understood the purpose  risks  and benefits of the procedure  The  patient confirmed name  date of birth  laterality  and lesion to be aspirated   Written consent was obtained     The biopsy procedure was performed by palpation     3 aspirates were performed using 25 gauge needles without local anesthesia and  without complications   3 slides were stained for immediate evaluation  Additional tissue was submitted  for further evaluation     The procedure was performed by  Aisagbonhi Omonigho A  M D     Disposition  The immediate interpretation was not discussed with the patient   Connie Roche  N P was notified of the preliminary results by personal  communication at 2 15 PM         The signing pathologist attests that he she performed the procedure or  if  performed by a resident or fellow  directed and supervised the FNA procedure and  was immediately available to further assist the resident fellow before  during  and after the procedure  and has reviewed and confirmed the interpretation of  slides stained for immediate evaluation           Electronically Signed Out By  Rosemary H Tambouret MD on REMOVED_DATE 07 15      CLINICAL HISTORY  Left breast IDC s p bilateral mastectomy and radiation  s p bilateral TRAM flap  now with painful left chest wall nodule x1 day in scar        SPECIMEN SOURCE  A  FNA BIOPSY  LEFT ANTERIOR CHEST WALL NODULE  INFERIOR TO BREAST