Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery  WACC 3 MEEI MRN  REMOVED_DATE Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 2 Results To  MICHELLE C SPECHT  M D  Signed Out by  Ronald Balassanian  M D  CLINICAL DATA  The patient is a 67 year old woman with a mass in the left breast undergoing needle localization lumpectomy  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  A  BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  1  INTRADUCTAL PAPILLOMA  REMOVED_DATE CM 2  FLORID USUAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA 3  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGE WITH SCLEROSING ADENOSIS AND APOCRINE METAPLASIA  SEE NOTE  NOTE  Sections of the left breast reveal a papillary duct lesion with a proliferation of ductal cells lining fibrovascular cores with a frond like growth pattern distending the surrounding duct structure  The lesion is focally disrupted and extends to the inked margin of resection in multiple foci  limiting the interpretation  The ductal cells are intermediate in size ranging from cuboidal to columnar with a moderate amount of cytoplasm and have round to oval nuclei  Scattered mitotic figures are present  Additionally  LCIS is noted adjacent to the papilloma in a single focus  A panel of immunohistochemical stains for cytokeratin  p63  myosin and calponin were performed on slides  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   The immunostaining pattern highlights the myoepithelial layer and supports the diagnosis  Immunostains for cytokeratin REMOVED_DATE and cytokeratin 14 were performed on slides  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and support the diagnosis of usual ductal hyperplasia  Overall  the findings are compatible with a papilloma with focal rupture  extending to the margin of resection  Dr  Dave Wilbur and Dr  Fredrick Koerner have reviewed this case and concur with the diagnosis  G