Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGINS  Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  LEE  KENNETH  ROBERT  M D   DIAGNOSIS  by LEE KENNETH ROBERT M D   LEFT BREAST  EXCISION FOR A MASS   INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  well differentiated  modified  Bloom Richardson grade I III   0 8cm  block 1   see note   No lymphovascular invasion is seen   No carcinoma in situ is seen  EIC negative    The invasive carcinoma is focally present at the margin   A granuloma is present adjacent to the carcinoma  suggestive of a prior  biopsy site   Immunoperoxidase studies are performed with the following results for  invasive carcinoma   Estrogen receptor NEGATIVE  Progesterone receptor NEGATIVE   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  neu NEGATIVE  0   Smooth muscle alpha actin NEGATIVE for myoepithelial cells  p63 NEGATIVE for myoepithelial cells   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   CALLA  NEGATIVE for myoepithelial cells  Note  The carcinoma has a very unusual appearance  The tumor nests are  organized in a lobular pattern with a circumscribed border  No  desmoplasia is present  Normal ducts with myoepithelial cells are present  within the carcinoma  The tumor cells are moderately atypical with  occasional mitotic figures   Although the H E appearance is very suggestive of a benign adenosis  lesion involved by hyperplasia  the complete absence of myoepithelial  cells using multiple markers for these cells and the encircling of normal  structures favors a diagnosis of invasive carcinoma   The patient has had prior excisions in this region in  REMOVED_CASE_ID    REMOVED_CASE_ID   and   REMOVED_CASE_ID   Review of these prior biopsies reveals a similar type of lesion  with a benign appearance on H E examination  However  given the results  of the current biopsy  these prior lesions are also probably an unusual  form of invasive carcinoma that has recurred locally   Because of the unusual nature of this case it was reviewed by  Dr  James Connolly and Dr  Stuart Schnitt who concur with this  interpretation   This carcinoma would be classified as AJCC  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID b NX MX   SURGICAL ADDENDUM  by LESTER SUSAN M D  PH D   FIRST SURGICAL ADDENDUM REMOVED_DATE DR  SUSAN LESTER  This case  as well as the prior three excisions  was reviewed by Dr  Frederick  Koerner at Massachusetts General Hospital  their number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   dated  11 6 02  with the following diagnosis    Malignant neoplasm  see note    Note  The neoplasm probably represents an unusual type of invasive  carcinoma  which cannot be precisely classified without additional  studies  The alternative diagnosis of carcinoma in situ involving  microglandular adenosis also cannot be excluded without additional studies   The lesion extends to the margin of the most recent specimen in several foci    The patient subsequently underwent a re excision at MGH with the following  results  their number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   11 19 02     LYMPH NODE  LEFT SENTINEL   EXCISION   RARE CARCINOMA CELLS ARE PRESENT IN 1 OF 1 LYMPH NODE   NOTE  A few tumor cells are present in a subcapsular sinus and are  detected on cytokeratin immunostains only  The involved lymph node is  0 4cm in greatest dimension  Only a single lymph node is identified in  the specimen   LEFT BREAST  RE EXCISION   INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  0 45 CM   GRADE 3   ATYPICAL GLANDULAR PROLIFERATION  SEE NOTE   ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA   LOBULAR NEOPLASIA  ATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA    HEALING BIOPSY SITE   NOTE  There is an unusual background proliferation of small glands with a  lobulated growth pattern  similar to that seen in the patient s prior breast  excisions  0 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID f REMOVED_CASE_ID    The epithelial cells in these glands are  cytologically atypical  mitotically active  and most are strongly positive for   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   These glands lack myoepithelial cells  as demonstrated on SMA  calponin    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  immunostains  but have basement membrane around them  as  detected on laminin immunostains   Eosinophilic luminal secretions are focally  present  Lesions with similar features have been termed  atypical  microglandular adenosis   but the possibility that these cases represent an  unusual form of invasive carcinoma cannot be excluded  In the current  specimen  the permeative growth and the absence of typical microglandular  adenosis favor a diagnosis of invasive carcinoma  Dr  Frederick Koerner has  reviewed the case and concurs with the interpretation  This glandular  proliferation spans approximately the entire length of the specimen  3 5cm     It is broadly present at the margin along the inferior half and is focally  present at the superior margin   Arising within this atypical proliferation is a nodule of high grade invasive  ductal carcinoma  which comes to 0 15cm from the inferior margin    Received from Massachusetts General Hospital are two paraffin blocks labeled   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and sublabeled  E  and  J  corresponding to the re excision specimen  and are recut for H E slides and immunoperoxidase studies   Immunoperoxidase studies are performed and the laminin studies are sent to MGH   Review of the two slides confirms that there is a small focus of definitively  invasive carcinoma surrounded by the atypical glandular proliferation   Myoepithelial cells are absent from the invasive carcinoma and the atypical  glandular proliferation by  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   The cells of the atypical glandular  proliferation show focally strong immunoreactivity for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   with more focal  weaker positivity within the invasive carcinoma  Most of the areas of the  glandular proliferation are surrounded by laminin  This is absent in the  invasive carcinoma   SECOND SURGICAL ADDENDUM REMOVED_DATE DR  SUSAN LESTER  Subsequently received  as part of BWH consult  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   are 10 H E slides  from the case described above   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    The slides are reviewed and I agree with the original diagnosis from MGH  No  lymphovascular invasion is present  The immunoperoxidase study on the lymph  node is not received and no tumor cells are seen on the H E slide of the node   as originally reported   Immunoperoxidase studies were performed on the 0 45cm carcinoma and reported  to show faint staining for estrogen and progesterone receptors and no  overexpression of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  neu  The surrounding cells in the atypical  proliferation were negative for all three markers   Immunoperoxidase studies are repeated at BWH at the request of Dr  Campos  No  immunoreactivity is seen for estrogen or progesterone receptors or  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  neu  0   in the 0 45cm carcinoma or in the surrounding atypical proliferation  Normal  epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for hormone receptors   CLINICAL DATA   History  Left breast mass   Operation  Removal left breast mass   Clinical Diagnosis  Left breast mass   TISSUE SUBMITTED  Left breast mass   GROSS DESCRIPTION  by HONG XUEFEI M D   The specimen is received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  left breast mass   and consists of one fragment of tan white  firm   rubbery soft tissue  2 8 x REMOVED_DATE x 0 8cm    The specimen is inked and entirely  submitted   Micro sections   Micro REMOVED_DATE  breast mass  multiple frags  ESS   tim  Reports to  REILLY  RAYMOND  JOHN  MB  SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGINS   report_end  , No.
